        "original_id": "c45e5320-8893-5eab-b2b2-960ff0d254a6",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants: Water dropwort, Costus, Anise\n- Genera: Oenanthe\n- Chemicals: Oenanthotoxin\n- Plant families: Apiaceae, Costaceae\n- Places: Eastern Mediterranean, Southwest Asia\n\nFacts:\n- In 1753, Linnaeus described Costus as a genus of perennial herbaceous plants in the family Costaceae.\n- Oenanthe is a genus of plants in the family Apiaceae and is known as water dropwort.\n- Oenanthotoxin is a toxin extracted from hemlock water dropwort, Oenanthe crocata, and other plants of the genus Oenanthe.\n- Anise, also called aniseed and scientifically named Pimpinella anisum, is a flowering plant in the family Apiaceae native to the eastern Mediterranean region and Southwest Asia.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "5cf06d6e-6c76-5e9f-b483-b95fccca881b",
        "text": "Page: Water dropwort. Sentence: The water dropworts, Oenanthe , are a genus of plants in the family Apiaceae.\nPage: Costus. Sentence: Costus is a group of perennial herbaceous plants in the family (Costaceae) described by Linnaeus as a genus in 1753.\nPage: Oenanthotoxin. Sentence: Oenanthotoxin is a toxin extracted from hemlock water dropwort (\"Oenanthe crocata\") and other plants of the genus \"Oenanthe\".\nPage: Anise. Sentence: Anise ( ; \"Pimpinella anisum \"), also called aniseed, is a flowering plant in the family Apiaceae native to the eastern Mediterranean region and Southwest Asia.",
